[#4471](/kb/incidents/4471) is marked known: one issue, one fix queued. Its reports describe more than
one problem, and the queued parser fix reaches only the first. Each problem now has its own ticket.

<ProblemTable ticket={4471} {tickets} />

<Callout kind="warn">
Shipping the parser fix does not close #4471's reports. The <code>hint</code> and <code>one-day</code>
reports keep coming until their own fixes ship.
</Callout>

## How each report was placed

1. One id, one report. The export repeats <ProblemCount ticket={4471} of="repeated" /> rows where its
   pages overlap; each counts once.
2. Every report is filed by its tenant, as the desk files it (`causes.ts`): a day-first tenant's report
   through booking-v3's form is `hint`; any other way in is `parser`.
3. The reports the desk read as a one-day move (`desk-notes.json`) are a problem of their own, so they
   have their own ticket. None of them shows a day and month swapped. As the desk counts them, they stay
   counted where they're filed too.
4. The intake tag is not used. It is a guess from the reporter's browser, and it names a different
   cause than the tenant does in <ProblemCount ticket={4471} of="tag-disagrees" /> reports.

## parser: day-first dates read month first

Reports: <ProblemCount ticket={4471} of="parser" />. The shared parser reads every date month first, so a
day-first tenant's 7 April becomes 4 July. Apart from the one-day moves, which the desk files here by
tenant, every report here that gives two dates shows the same day and month swapped, and one with no dates
asks for a "my country writes the day first" setting.
This is the problem the queued fix is for.

## hint: booking-v3 asks day-first tenants for month first

Reports: <ProblemCount ticket={4471} of="hint" />, all through booking-v3's form at day-first tenants.
The form prints `MM/DD/YYYY` under its date field, people type the month first as told, and the form reads
the date the tenant's way. The dates come out swapped like the parser's, but the parser fix does not touch
the form. #4471's own table marks these "not this ticket — no ticket of its own". The fix is a hint in the
tenant's order.

## one-day: dates move by one day

Reports: <ProblemCount ticket={4471} of="one-day" />. Nothing is swapped here; the day moves by one.

<ReportList ticket={4471} problem="one-day" />

A client in Toronto and a client in Lisbon got different days for the same meeting, and an invite arrived
before its booking existed: the day depends on the time zone it is shown in. The parser fix does not touch
this either. #4471's table files these under `parser` and leaves a footnote; they stay counted there,
and this ticket counts them as what they are.

## Subject lines do not tell them apart

<SubjectSplit ticket={4471} />

Decision [0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject) merges on subject alone. Most subjects arrive
for more than one problem, so no subject rule could have kept them apart. 0001 is accepted and stays as
written; a new decision record replaces it.

## On the desk

- File a date report by its tenant, its way in and its body, not by its subject or intake tag.
- Before closing a problem's ticket, check that its count on this page has stopped growing.

